For a 12 to 17 year old, a Trump Account is usually a weak choice, because its value depends on years of growth. The money is locked until January 1 of the year the child turns 18, so a 15 year old gets under three years of tax-deferred growth, a much weaker case than for a newborn. Withdrawals are then taxed as ordinary income.

An older child gets no $1,000 seed. A child born 2016 through 2024 may still receive the [$250 Dell deposit](/guides/dell-trump-account-250/), with no application needed, if the family lives in a ZIP code with median family income of about $118,000 or less. Compare the [alternatives](/guides/trump-account-alternatives/) first: a custodial Roth IRA grows tax-free if the teen has earned income, a [529](/compare/trump-account-vs-529/) is tax-free for school, and a custodial brokerage account has no lock-up. For a teen, open a Trump Account to collect the Dell $250, and put larger savings in the alternative that fits the goal.